import Foundation

public struct AIKnowledgePack: Codable, Sendable {
    public let version: String
    public let notes: String
    public func validate() throws {
        guard version.range(of: "^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,80}$", options: .regularExpression) != nil,
              !notes.isEmpty, notes.utf8.count <= 24576 else { throw AIError.message("Knowledge pack requires a version and 1-24576 bytes of notes") }
    }
    public static let foundation = """
    Kiln is a modular native Swift application platform by P.Industries. The supervisor manages local applications, with optional explicitly adopted peer nodes for remote administration.
    Deployed application modules are separate trusted OS processes identified by application/module. Swift framework targets remain linked into the host executable.
    Available: optional public-key SSH, native TUI, persistent remote terminal service, bastion destinations, host metrics, local executable manifests, start/stop/restart, HTTP readiness, loopback TCP ingress, draining, rollback, encrypted Vault and per-module startup grants.
    A managed release stages an executable, starts a candidate on a distinct worker port, requires three HTTP 200 health probes, persists active/previous manifests, switches new TCP connections, drains old connections for up to 30 seconds and stops the old worker. Existing WebSockets do not migrate; clients need reconnect after forced drain. Rollback restores code, not database writes. Legacy app restart interrupts that app. Supervisor restart interrupts app traffic. Remote terminals live in a separate service and survive a dashboard restart while that service remains alive.
    Module manifests: application, name, version, executable (absolute local path), arguments, optional healthURL and ingressPort, optional secrets mapping environment variables to Vault references, optional restartPolicy (never or always), optional Linux resources budgets (memoryBytes, cpuMillis, maxProcesses). Budgets apply separately to each concurrent worker generation, not an aggregate application. Requested hard budgets fail closed when unsupported; macOS does not enforce Linux cgroups. The supervisor supplies KILN_APP_NAME, KILN_DATA_DIR, KILN_RELEASE_VERSION and KILN_PORT for managed ingress. Versions use YY.MM.DD.BUILD for Kiln releases.
    Vault uses authenticated encryption and an external master-key file. No secret reveal tool exists. Per-module grants govern startup delivery; running processes retain received values. Same-UID modules are trusted, not sandboxed. AI credentials use profile-scoped grants: kiln/ai for the default profile and kiln/ai-PROFILE for named profiles. Never request or disclose credentials in chat.
    Native AI provider profiles support Erebine, local Ollama, OpenAI Responses, Claude Messages, Grok Responses and DeepSeek Chat Completions. API-key profiles remain separate from account sign-in. ChatGPT account sign-in uses the official OSS OAuth/PKCE flow, encrypted Vault tokens, owner identity plus SSH-key fingerprint, live model discovery and a separate chatgpt profile. Grok CLI account sign-in uses its official device flow in an isolated home. Both require user consent and confirmed provider entitlement. Current sign-in and model access must be verified with account results; historical fixture or preview acceptance is not proof of the current connection. Never claim an account is connected without a verified result. Provider changes require a fresh context, with no automatic fallback. Claude and DeepSeek thinking is disabled in these adapters. An experimental administrator-only Grok CLI inference worker uses sandbox-exec on macOS or bubblewrap with working user namespaces on Linux. API-key mode uses a separate kiln/ai-grok-cli grant; device sign-in accounts use encrypted account credentials. It has no Kiln tools or host workspace. CLI authenticated inference requires operator verification. Native Erebine and local Ollama passed real read-tool, streamed-answer and cancellation acceptance; OpenAI/Claude/Grok/DeepSeek API-key adapters currently have fixture validation without available live credentials.
    Named SSH identities have admin/operator/viewer roles and optional exact application/module scopes. Authorization is checked again on commands and AI reads/approvals; chat cannot broaden permission. Background tasks and cluster administration require administrator access. Revocation blocks subsequent management requests. Local host access and trusted same-UID application executables are outside management-plane RBAC.
    Optional peer-node management uses pinned Ed25519 SSH identities. Discovery is untrusted; both sides must explicitly adopt fingerprints before typed operations. Revoke blocks subsequent peer requests. Repeated request IDs do not blindly replay mutations. Remote peer operations are not automatic placement, shared storage, quorum or failover.
    KilnWeb is implemented: independently compiled Swift HTTP workers, escaped HTML templates, controller routes and server actions, session/Origin/CSRF checks, WebSocket LiveView events and application-local invalidation via LiveHub. Explicit development reload refreshes HTML on worker instance change. The macOS-only registered development-source path gives foreground administrators a bounded coding workflow: discover projects/tools, read source, isolated compile-only validation, exact source edits, healthy candidate deployment, source persistence, durable receipts and same-origin HTTP preview checks. Read tool_help(topic: web) for exact APIs, constraints and repair workflow. This is one registered source file per app, not arbitrary repository, dependency, shell or production-publishing access. Validating edits does not publish them. Runtime sandboxing applies specifically to edited development workers, not every ordinary installed app. Black and green is the default brand; colors remain customizable. Portable bundles are deferred.
    Planned, NOT IMPLEMENTED: automatic cluster scheduling/failover, shared replicated state, custom database, public TLS/ACME, Cloudflare DNS/Tunnel, Resend, independent replacement of SSH/ingress platform processes, Git builds/downloads, native Apple management applications. Do not claim those are available.
    Running is process state, not proof of health. Use health tools for actual checks. restartPolicy always enables three automatic retries with 1/2/4-second backoff; 60 seconds of continuous process uptime resets the budget. It does not prove health. Explicit start resets the budget; stop disables recovery. Omitted policies require manual restart. AI operations have persistent outcome receipts (ai operations); unconfirmed outcomes must be inspected and must not be blindly retried. Ordinary chat approvals remain session-bound and in memory; durable recovery tasks retain owner-bound restart approvals and evidence across reconnects, with expiry and generation checks. Direct workers have guardians that terminate them if the supervisor dies; startup reconciles ownership before reopening SSH and restores the committed active release. This interrupts ingress connections, does not migrate them, and does not contain daemonized descendants or guardian SIGKILL for unconstrained workers. Linux cgroup-constrained workers include descendant cleanup; this is not a sandbox against malicious same-UID code. Interrupted background tasks are not replayed; unfinished operation receipts remain unconfirmed. There is no multi-node failover. macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 (Swift 6.2.3 container) passed release tests and SSH/REST/WebSocket runtime integration. Separate peer tests verify explicit adoption and remote administration. These checks do not establish production deployment, every Linux distribution, consensus, scheduling or failover.
    """
}
